m to other columns.\n\nWe have columns 1,...,n-4 left (n-4 columns). We have n-4 non-sunny lines left, which can be vertical lines for each of those columns. So we can assign vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4. So we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4, n-3, n-2. That's n-2 vertical lines total, covering all columns except n-1 and n. Then we have 2 sunny lines to cover points in columns n-1 and n.\n\nNow we need to check if we can cover points in column n-1 (2 points) and column n (1 point) with 2 sunny lines, respecting the restriction that a sunny line cannot be horizontal or slope -1.\n\nWe have points: (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). We need to assign each point to a sunny line, with each line covering at most one point per column. So we need to assign (n,1) to one line, and (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) to the two lines, but we cannot assign both (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) to the same line because they are in same column. So each of the two sunny lines must cover exactly one point from column n-1, and one of them must also cover (n,1). So one sunny line will cover (n,1) and one of the points in column n-1. Let's see if we can find a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and (n-1,1) or (n-1,2). As we saw, line through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (y=1), not allowed. Line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope -1, not allowed. So (n,1) cannot be paired with any point from column n-1 on a sunny line. Therefore, (n,1) must be on a sunny line by itself (i.e., the line must pass through (n,1) and some other point not in column n-1). But we have only 2 sunny lines, and we need to cover both points in column n-1. So we need a third sunny line to cover (n,1) and one of the points in column n-1, but that's impossible. So we need at least 3 sunny lines. Therefore, k=2 is impossible.\n\nThus, the answer set {0,1,3} seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to check if k=4 is possible. Let's see if we can cover column n-1 and n with 4 sunny lines? Actually we need to consider the minimal number of su